WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.840
Have you ever asked yourself how a robot makes a decision how to go from its current location

00:06.840 --> 00:08.620
to a target location?

00:08.620 --> 00:14.180
Then this lecture is for you because we will discuss motion planning for mobile robots.

00:14.180 --> 00:20.000
So how do robots make decisions where to navigate in order to reach a target location?

00:20.000 --> 00:24.940
So typically a robot knows where it is, it knows where it should go to, it has a map

00:24.940 --> 00:30.880
of the environment and knows something about its own shape or own body form so that it

00:30.880 --> 00:36.540
knows how it can execute local motion commands such as go a meter forward and that the system

00:36.540 --> 00:42.080
will execute that without bumping into walls next to the robot for example.

00:42.080 --> 00:45.800
And then a motion planning algorithm generates a sequence of motion commands or a sequence

00:45.800 --> 00:50.660
of states that the robot needs to visit in order to reach the target location.

00:50.660 --> 00:53.040
And it can do so in an optimal fashion.

00:53.080 --> 00:58.280
An optimal fashion means there's no better way for doing this in terms of the output result.

00:58.280 --> 01:04.140
So there's no better trajectory than the trajectory that the algorithm actually proposes.

01:04.140 --> 01:09.000
And of course this optimality as always comes with some assumptions such as which stage

01:09.000 --> 01:14.300
can we visit, do we discretize our space, but that's something that we're going to explore

01:14.300 --> 01:16.060
in this lecture today.

01:16.060 --> 01:22.600
So typically we are interested in computing a path or trajectory which guides the robot

01:22.660 --> 01:26.360
on its fastest route to its goal location.

01:26.360 --> 01:31.300
And that's what we are going to discuss today, how do we make this decision where to navigate

01:31.300 --> 01:33.220
in order to reach our goal.

01:33.220 --> 01:37.220
So that can be a robot such as the one shown here on that slide which navigates through

01:37.220 --> 01:42.860
the catacombs under Rome and needs to reach a certain location in those catacombs but

01:42.860 --> 01:49.040
can be also any other type of robot or even yourself if you use your sub-nav system in

01:49.040 --> 01:52.420
the car and the system tells you where to navigate.

01:52.420 --> 01:59.060
You can do this with an algorithm called A*, even though in devices used today there may

01:59.060 --> 02:05.780
be some more advanced or optimized algorithms for the problem at hand, but the basic motion

02:05.780 --> 02:11.160
planning algorithm that underlies this can be solved with an A*.

02:11.160 --> 02:18.380
So more concretely we are looking today into robot motion planning using the A*, and discuss

02:18.380 --> 02:25.200
what A*, means, how it works, and how we can use this algorithm in order to build real

02:25.200 --> 02:27.720
robots that navigate through the world.

02:27.720 --> 02:34.360
So the motion planning problem is a standard problem in robotics since its beginnings,

02:34.360 --> 02:40.120
so there's a famous quote made by Latombe in 1991 which states that motion planning

02:40.120 --> 02:45.440
is an essential task that robots need to do because in order to do something useful most

02:45.440 --> 02:49.820
of the robotic systems, at least of the mobile robotic systems, need to move through the

02:49.820 --> 02:55.260
environment from A to B in order to succeed, in order to fulfill its task.

02:55.260 --> 03:01.860
So moving through the environment is a core ingredient of most robotic systems today and

03:01.860 --> 03:08.540
therefore motion planning or path planning is an important capability for those systems.

03:08.540 --> 03:14.620
If we look to the, let's call it, classical motion planning and what are the traditional

03:14.620 --> 03:19.460
goals that we always look into, so the first thing we want to ensure is that the robot

03:19.460 --> 03:21.700
never collides with an object.

03:21.700 --> 03:26.580
So if we have walls or doors in the environment we want to make sure the robot will never

03:26.580 --> 03:32.200
bump into a wall, always fit through the door frame and without touching on the right or

03:32.200 --> 03:37.880
on the left-hand side the door frame or collide with a door or any other object in the space.

03:37.880 --> 03:42.640
So collision-free trajectory is a requirement that we typically have.

03:42.640 --> 03:49.460
And among this set of collision-free trajectories from a start location to a goal location,

03:49.460 --> 03:54.780
we are typically interested in finding the trajectory which guide the robots on the shortest

03:54.780 --> 04:00.260
path or that the robot can reach its target location as quickly as possible.

04:00.260 --> 04:06.460
So the shortest path is not always the fastest path, so there can be trajectories along which

04:06.460 --> 04:11.500
you can, which are longer, but along which you can drive faster, so you may take a small

04:11.500 --> 04:16.080
detour into account if you can drive faster on that trajectory.

04:16.080 --> 04:20.880
It's similar to you when you're sitting in your car and there are two roads you can take,

04:20.880 --> 04:24.640
the one is a highway and the other one is a local street to the city.

04:24.640 --> 04:29.320
Even though the trajectory along the highway is somewhat longer, you can drive faster on

04:29.320 --> 04:32.880
that highway and therefore will reach your target location faster.

04:32.880 --> 04:39.480
So for several applications we are interested in the fastest route, but there are also applications

04:39.480 --> 04:42.240
where we may be interested in the shortest path.

04:42.240 --> 04:45.900
Often they are similar, but not necessarily the same.

04:45.900 --> 04:52.040
So the traditional goal in robot motion planning is provide a collision-free trajectory that

04:52.040 --> 04:56.000
allows us to reach the goal as quickly as possible.

04:56.000 --> 04:58.020
This is often formulated in static worlds.

04:58.020 --> 05:03.320
That means the robot knows about the world and also knows that the world does not change.

05:03.320 --> 05:07.560
That means there are no dynamic obstacles around, there are no humans moving around,

05:07.560 --> 05:10.740
no other traffic participants for example.

05:10.740 --> 05:15.520
So this is something which is a very strong assumption and typically not justified in

05:15.520 --> 05:16.660
the real world.

05:16.660 --> 05:21.620
So whenever we go to real world robotics we need to take dynamic environments into account.

05:21.620 --> 05:27.360
That means environments that change while the robot is operating in this environment.

05:27.360 --> 05:31.500
These can be quickly moving obstacles like other traffic participants, but this could

05:31.500 --> 05:36.220
also mean that there are objects which are placed in the environment that hadn't been

05:36.220 --> 05:40.260
around when the robot was building its map for example, or just data that's not stored

05:40.260 --> 05:41.260
in the map in general.

05:41.260 --> 05:43.000
And how can we deal with this?

05:43.000 --> 05:50.580
So how can we still navigate effectively, have an efficient algorithm that computes

05:50.580 --> 05:56.780
a path quickly and get out a path which guides us to the target location as quickly as possible

05:56.780 --> 06:01.740
and that we can still give some guarantees, at least guarantees under some assumptions

06:01.740 --> 06:09.460
that the robot will not bump into an object even as an object it didn't know before.

06:09.460 --> 06:13.120
In order to do that of course we typically need to make some assumptions how objects

06:13.120 --> 06:18.020
move through the environment, if the object is much faster than the robot, basically chases

06:18.020 --> 06:23.720
the robot, of course this can lead to a collision, a collision we cannot avoid.

06:23.720 --> 06:30.420
But if we make certain assumptions about the movements of the other objects in the environment

06:30.420 --> 06:36.320
we can make certain statements at least probabilistic statements if this trajectory will lead to

06:36.320 --> 06:37.840
collision yes or no.

06:37.840 --> 06:43.180
So we are interested in finding trajectories that are reliable and that we can efficiently

06:43.180 --> 06:44.540
navigate on.

06:44.540 --> 06:50.700
And there have been a lot of techniques proposed in order to deal with those dynamic environments.

06:50.700 --> 06:54.300
Often we have to distinguish two things in here, a core planning algorithm that plans

06:54.300 --> 06:59.700
general paths from A to B and then what we call like a local collision avoidance systems

06:59.700 --> 07:04.000
that tries to follow that path but is allowed to deviate from the path in order to avoid

07:04.000 --> 07:05.340
collisions.

07:05.340 --> 07:10.540
And prominent examples are the dynamic window approaches which are techniques which basically

07:10.540 --> 07:15.300
take into account which changes in the velocities the system can do and this generates your

07:15.300 --> 07:21.620
local window of states that you can reach and then the question is which is the best

07:21.620 --> 07:24.140
velocity to choose in that window.

07:24.140 --> 07:29.260
So you're basically choosing the velocity commands that bring you close to the goal

07:29.260 --> 07:32.180
or along the trajectory towards the goal.

07:32.180 --> 07:37.260
And there is a large series of other approaches, some for example optimized for very confined

07:37.260 --> 07:43.380
spaces so where it's very hard for the robot to navigate, others more optimized through

07:43.380 --> 07:51.640
environments where you can go as fast as possible and can make smooth avoidance maneuvers around

07:51.640 --> 07:57.840
optical objects in the world or that make some assumptions regarding optimal or near

07:57.840 --> 08:00.760
optimal behavior while navigating through the environment.

08:00.760 --> 08:04.980
So it depends a little bit on the approach that you want to do and the goals and your

08:04.980 --> 08:07.100
environment that you are in.

08:07.100 --> 08:12.140
So we will be looking today especially here into the so-called A-star algorithm which

08:12.140 --> 08:17.480
is a very popular search algorithm, a so-called informed search algorithm that is used for

08:17.480 --> 08:21.200
a large variety of different planning problems, not only in robotics.

08:21.240 --> 08:26.840
It has been developed for robotics and is again one of the key AI techniques that we

08:26.840 --> 08:31.400
use today, that we find today in a lot of applications and it's kind of the gold standard

08:31.400 --> 08:32.400
planning method.

08:32.400 --> 08:36.880
So it's kind of important to understand how that approach works because it allows you

08:36.880 --> 08:41.180
to generate a sequence of states that you need to visit to go from a star to a goal

08:41.180 --> 08:45.400
location and it does so in a very effective manner.

08:45.400 --> 08:50.440
There again extensions and variants of the A-star algorithm such as ARA-star, Anytime

08:50.680 --> 08:55.920
Real-time A-star for example which is a real-time algorithm that takes the computational resources

08:55.920 --> 09:01.520
or an anytime algorithm sorry that takes the computational resources into account in order

09:01.520 --> 09:07.640
to optimize the trajectory but guarantees or not guarantees but in most of the settings

09:07.640 --> 09:12.920
comes up with an initial trajectory very quickly that you can start navigating on and then

09:12.920 --> 09:19.160
optimize this trajectory on the way or the D-star family of algorithms which can be used

09:19.160 --> 09:20.600
if you do a lot of replanning.

09:20.600 --> 09:25.280
So if you don't do a plan just once but replan while the environment is changing and then

09:25.280 --> 09:30.600
they allow you for substantially more efficient updates.

09:30.600 --> 09:35.160
And then there are also other approaches like Markov decision processes which you then need

09:35.160 --> 09:41.080
to take into account if the uncertainty in your motion execution plays an important role.

09:41.080 --> 09:45.680
So if you assume the system can actually execute what it wants to execute then you're typically

09:45.680 --> 09:52.120
fine with your A-star algorithm but if you have uncertainties in your motion execution

09:52.120 --> 09:57.240
you have to go for other things other techniques such as MDPs for example.

09:57.240 --> 10:01.320
So let's have a look what are the key challenges today in motion planning.

10:01.320 --> 10:05.660
So what the key challenges are we want to find an optimal path.

10:05.660 --> 10:08.240
Optimal means under the cost functions that I'm providing.

10:08.240 --> 10:14.440
This can be shortest paths, shortest collision-free paths, fastest paths, a path or trajectory

10:14.440 --> 10:21.360
which minimizes certain accelerations that occur that are very pleasant for humans if

10:21.360 --> 10:25.000
humans are driven by the autonomous vehicle like an autonomous car.

10:25.000 --> 10:28.400
You just want to have the fastest trajectory, you want to have also trajectory which is

10:28.400 --> 10:33.600
for example legal or a trajectory that is very convenient to when you're sitting in

10:33.600 --> 10:38.920
the car and you don't care if you arrive at the goal two or three seconds slower as long

10:38.920 --> 10:41.000
as your ride is more pleasant for example.

10:41.000 --> 10:46.400
So there may be different cost functions that you want to take into account here in

10:46.400 --> 10:51.740
this lecture we are basically looking into the shortest path or the fastest path.

10:51.740 --> 10:55.140
Then the second question is do you need to take into account uncertainties?

10:55.140 --> 11:01.360
These can be uncertainties in the action execution so when you go from A to B and you give a

11:01.360 --> 11:07.480
command to the system is the command actually being executed or is something else executed?

11:07.480 --> 11:12.320
So for example if you say go a meter forward and the system goes on the 95 centimeters

11:12.320 --> 11:16.320
forward is this being taken into account?

11:16.320 --> 11:18.380
Is this actually a problem for you?

11:18.380 --> 11:23.080
So this is something that needs to be considered and you may also take into account uncertainties

11:23.080 --> 11:27.280
in your perception system that your perception system is not perfect.

11:27.280 --> 11:31.080
The other question is how can you quickly react to unforeseen obstacles?

11:31.080 --> 11:37.480
So if a new obstacle is in your path something that you are not aware of how quickly can

11:37.480 --> 11:40.320
you react to these unforeseen obstacles?

11:40.320 --> 11:44.600
Or what happens if other traffic participants are moving through the environment and may

11:44.600 --> 11:46.680
collide with you even if you would stop?

11:46.680 --> 11:51.600
So these are things that your planning system may take into account but that somehow depends

11:51.600 --> 11:54.320
on the application you're actually targeting.

11:54.320 --> 12:01.880
If we look into most robotics navigation stacks we can see a so-called layered architecture

12:01.880 --> 12:09.600
so you have different layers of planning until control algorithms which are executed by the

12:09.600 --> 12:13.400
system running at different frequencies and having different tasks that the system is

12:13.400 --> 12:14.400
going to do.

12:14.400 --> 12:19.020
So what you can see here is a classical three layered architecture on the highest level

12:19.020 --> 12:23.420
you have your planning algorithm so that's something that we are starting with here with

12:23.420 --> 12:24.960
our ACE algorithm.

12:24.960 --> 12:30.460
So how to use a map of the environment and the information where you are in the world

12:30.460 --> 12:35.220
in order to plan a path that guides the robot to its target location.

12:35.220 --> 12:40.060
Maybe you do that once per second or once every five seconds or maybe three times per

12:40.060 --> 12:46.640
second so but at a rather low frequency you're basically reviving your plan based on the

12:46.640 --> 12:50.500
new estimates where you are in the environment and what happens around you.

12:50.500 --> 12:56.620
And then you typically communicate a sub-goal so a sub-goal let's say five meters in front

12:56.620 --> 13:01.280
of a robot to our local planning system a collision avoidance system and the collision

13:01.280 --> 13:05.880
avoidance system runs at a much higher frequency typically at the frame rate of your sensor

13:05.880 --> 13:13.160
so this can be something from five hertz to 30 hertz or 50 hertz maybe even checking if

13:13.160 --> 13:19.720
there is an obstacle that moves near the path that the system wants to execute so that there's

13:19.740 --> 13:24.640
a risk for a collision and if it sees that there's a risk that the robot would collide

13:24.640 --> 13:29.900
with an obstacle it plans or varies a trajectory and generates alternative trajectories that

13:29.900 --> 13:33.500
the system then picks in order to avoid a collision that's something that we often refer

13:33.500 --> 13:37.240
to as a collision avoidance system or a local planner.

13:37.240 --> 13:42.580
And then this collision avoidance system actually generates typically a motion command or the

13:42.580 --> 13:48.420
transition to a next state and then you have a local controller that turns this local information

13:48.420 --> 13:54.480
into an actual steering command or control the motors of the vehicle so that the vehicle

13:54.480 --> 13:59.880
actually executes the path that or the the motion command it should execute and typically

13:59.880 --> 14:03.960
there's also a correcting control loop that for example odometers are taken into account

14:03.960 --> 14:08.480
so a sensor that counts the revolution of the wheels in order to make sure the platform

14:08.480 --> 14:13.240
actually executes what it should execute and take some corrective actions.

14:13.240 --> 14:16.840
So today we will be looking more on the planning side looking a little bit also into collision

14:16.860 --> 14:20.500
avoidance or a system which solved these two tasks jointly.

